# The RUNS bucket — documentation code blocks this suite executes and that exit 0.
#
# Read by `tests/doc_blocks_test.rs`. Every ```redblue block in SPEC.md,
# README.md, PHILOSOPHY.md, HANDOUT.md and docs/GRAMMAR.md must appear in
# exactly one of this file, `tests/doc_blocks_fragments.txt` and
# `tests/doc_blocks_known_fail.txt`. A block in none of them fails the suite.
#
# Being listed HERE is a promise the suite keeps by running the block: if one of
# these stops exiting 0, `running_every_block_agrees_with_the_bucket_it_is_in`
# fails and the block has to move to the KNOWN-FAIL file, with the first line of
# stderr it now prints. That is the whole point of listing the working blocks
# at all: without this file a block that stopped working would silently join the
# fragments, and a documentation promise would rot without a word.
#
# "Runs" means BOTH runners now. `rb run` has to exit 0 AND `rb compile` has to
# exit 0. `SPEC.md:647` sat in this file for a whole phase while `rb compile`
# refused the function literal it is written with — "`to (x) ... end` as an
# expression is not compiled yet" — and no gate in the suite could see it,
# because `rb run` never asked the compiler anything.
#
===
# The KNOWN-FAIL bucket — documentation blocks that are whole programs and
# still fail today.
#
# Read by `tests/doc_blocks_test.rs`. Every ```redblue block in SPEC.md,
# README.md, PHILOSOPHY.md, HANDOUT.md and docs/GRAMMAR.md must appear in
# exactly one of this file and `tests/doc_blocks_fragments.txt`. A block in
# neither fails the suite.
#
# WHAT KNOWN-FAIL MEANS
# The block is a program — every name it reads is bound somewhere above it in
# the same block — and this suite wrote it to a file and ran it, and it exited
# non-zero. The second column is the exact first line of stderr, so an entry
# goes stale the moment the behaviour changes: fixing any of these without
# re-running the suite fails here, and so does the failure moving, or changing
# its wording, or being reclassified as a fragment.
#
# This is the list a human can act on. Each entry is a sentence in a document
# that `rb` contradicts. Nothing here is implemented by this phase and nothing
# here is fixed by it: AGENTS.md § 1.7, one phase one concern.
#
===
# The FRAGMENT bucket — documentation code blocks this suite does not execute.
#
# Read by `tests/doc_blocks_test.rs`. Every ```redblue block in SPEC.md,
# README.md, PHILOSOPHY.md, HANDOUT.md and docs/GRAMMAR.md must appear in
# exactly one of `this file` and `tests/doc_blocks_known_fail.txt`. A block in
# neither fails the suite, so neither list can quietly shrink.
#
# WHAT FRAGMENT MEANS
# The block is not executed, and the reason is written beside it. Two kinds of
# reason appear here, and they are not the same thing:
#
#   excerpt - the block is not a program. It has an elided `end`, a placeholder
#             body (`// statements`, `// ...`, `// Implementation`), or names
#             that only the surrounding prose introduces. Nothing about running
